1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:03,140
Pre-flight doesn't run as one big audit at the end.

2
00:00:03,140 --> 00:00:07,461
It's a pass, then another pass, on the same batch, and rights comes right after

3
00:00:07,461 --> 00:00:07,844
claims.

4
00:00:07,844 --> 00:00:12,439
Section 4.1 already taught Sam one version of this problem: an avatar's licence tier

5
00:00:12,439 --> 00:00:13,752
quietly gating paid use.

6
00:00:13,752 --> 00:00:15,557
That was never the whole picture.

7
00:00:15,557 --> 00:00:20,042
It was the first, most obvious piece of a question that applies to everything in a

8
00:00:20,042 --> 00:00:21,902
frame that isn't your own product.

9
00:00:21,902 --> 00:00:26,442
Four things in a typical ad can carry a rights problem: a face, a voice, a piece of

10
00:00:26,442 --> 00:00:28,412
music, and a brand that isn't yours.

11
00:00:28,412 --> 00:00:30,435
You've handled the first two already.

12
00:00:30,435 --> 00:00:33,718
This lecture gathers all four, and adds the two you haven't.

13
00:00:33,718 --> 00:00:38,094
Why gather them into one lecture instead of leaving each where it first came up?

14
00:00:38,094 --> 00:00:42,688
A checklist scattered across three sections is one nobody actually runs in full, and

15
00:00:42,688 --> 00:00:47,229
remembering under deadline pressure is exactly the failure mode this section exists

16
00:00:47,229 --> 00:00:47,830
to prevent.

17
00:00:47,830 --> 00:00:50,675
One place, four questions, run together, every time.

18
00:00:50,675 --> 00:00:53,300
Notice, too, that all four share the same shape.

19
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:57,786
Each one asks whether something in your frame belongs to someone else, and whether

20
00:00:57,786 --> 00:00:59,427
you have permission to use it.

21
00:00:59,427 --> 00:01:03,967
Once you see that pattern, you stop needing a separate category for "avatar rights"

22
00:01:03,967 --> 00:01:08,179
versus "music rights" versus "brand rights." It's one question, asked of four

23
00:01:08,179 --> 00:01:08,562
things.

24
00:01:09,112 --> 00:01:11,412
Notice the last row isn't actually new.

25
00:01:11,412 --> 00:01:16,130
An AI-generated actor is the same category as the avatars you already checked in

26
00:01:16,130 --> 00:01:18,489
Section 4.1, whatever the tool calls it.

27
00:01:18,489 --> 00:01:23,384
The genuinely new material here is music and third-party brands, because nothing so

28
00:01:23,384 --> 00:01:25,979
far has asked you to think about either one.

29
00:01:25,979 --> 00:01:30,874
Music is the easiest to get wrong, because a track that's free to use personally is

30
00:01:30,874 --> 00:01:34,530
often not automatically cleared for paid commercial placement.

31
00:01:34,530 --> 00:01:39,425
Check the specific licence tier a platform's music library grants for ads, the same

32
00:01:39,425 --> 00:01:44,025
discipline Section 4.1 already taught you for avatars, applied to a completely

33
00:01:44,025 --> 00:01:44,969
different asset.

34
00:01:44,969 --> 00:01:49,864
Ask yourself, honestly: have you ever actually read a music library's licence terms

35
00:01:49,864 --> 00:01:54,287
all the way through, or did you assume "free" meant cleared for everything?

36
00:01:54,287 --> 00:01:55,408
Most people assume.

37
00:01:55,408 --> 00:02:00,126
That assumption is exactly where a rights gap on music actually starts, quietly,

38
00:02:00,126 --> 00:02:01,836
months before anyone notices.

39
00:02:02,386 --> 00:02:07,095
Here's what running all four actually looks like on a real batch, not four separate

40
00:02:07,095 --> 00:02:09,365
audits scattered across different weeks.

41
00:02:09,365 --> 00:02:12,429
Sam runs this as one pass, right before a batch ships.

42
00:02:12,429 --> 00:02:16,344
The face and voice check is the one he already had, from Section 4.1.

43
00:02:16,344 --> 00:02:21,110
Music gets the same treatment: not "is this free," but "is this specifically cleared

44
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:25,536
for paid social," which is a different, narrower question a lot of free-to-use

45
00:02:25,536 --> 00:02:27,352
libraries don't actually answer.

46
00:02:27,352 --> 00:02:32,118
And every generated scene gets one visual sweep for a brand that shouldn't be there,

47
00:02:32,118 --> 00:02:36,827
the same scan Section 3.3 taught him to run for his own product, aimed outward this

48
00:02:36,827 --> 00:02:37,111
time.

49
00:02:37,111 --> 00:02:41,877
Total time for all four checks on a full batch: about ten minutes, once it's a habit

50
00:02:41,877 --> 00:02:44,260
rather than something relearned each time.

51
00:02:44,260 --> 00:02:48,743
Compare that to the cost of any single one going wrong, a takedown, a licensing

52
00:02:48,743 --> 00:02:53,509
dispute, a legal letter from a brand whose logo showed up uninvited, and ten minutes

53
00:02:53,509 --> 00:02:55,098
stops looking like overhead.

54
00:02:55,098 --> 00:02:58,502
It's some of the cheapest insurance in this entire workflow.

55
00:02:59,052 --> 00:03:03,648
One trap worth naming plainly, because it's easy to walk into without noticing.

56
00:03:03,648 --> 00:03:07,895
A generator asked for "a person doing laundry" might return a shot with a

57
00:03:07,895 --> 00:03:12,781
recognisable detergent bottle in frame, purely because that's a common object in its

58
00:03:12,781 --> 00:03:16,039
training data, not because anyone chose it deliberately.

59
00:03:16,039 --> 00:03:20,402
If that bottle is a real, identifiable brand, you've now got someone else's

60
00:03:20,402 --> 00:03:23,020
trademark in your ad without their knowledge.

61
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:27,616
Check generated scenes for exactly this, the same way Section 3.3 taught you to

62
00:03:27,616 --> 00:03:31,921
check your own product for accuracy, just aimed outward instead of inward.

63
00:03:31,921 --> 00:03:33,783
Why does this feel easy to skip?

64
00:03:33,783 --> 00:03:38,320
Because your attention, checking a generated scene, naturally goes to your own

65
00:03:38,320 --> 00:03:41,520
product first, the way Section 3.3 trained you to look.

66
00:03:41,520 --> 00:03:46,407
A background brand sits outside that focus entirely, in the part of the frame you're

67
00:03:46,407 --> 00:03:48,501
least likely to be studying closely.

68
00:03:48,501 --> 00:03:53,330
That's exactly why it needs its own deliberate glance, not a hope that you'd notice

69
00:03:53,330 --> 00:03:54,144
it in passing.

70
00:03:54,694 --> 00:03:59,238
Next lecture asks the question this whole section has been building toward: once all

71
00:03:59,238 --> 00:04:03,782
four checks exist, who actually runs them, and at what point in the process, so they

72
00:04:03,782 --> 00:04:06,865
happen every time instead of only when someone remembers.

73
00:04:06,865 --> 00:04:11,300
Before that, run all four checks on whatever's currently live in your own account,

74
00:04:11,300 --> 00:04:13,464
not just the batch you're about to ship.

75
00:04:13,464 --> 00:04:18,008
It's a fair bet at least one older asset is running on a music track or a background

76
00:04:18,008 --> 00:04:22,444
scene you never actually checked this closely, and it's better to find that out on

77
00:04:22,444 --> 00:04:24,661
your own schedule than on someone else's.
